Using Moore's algorithm:-(optimal)

Approach:-

1.The majority element appears more than n/2 times.

2.Think of every occurrence of the majority element as getting +1 vote, and every different element as getting -1 vote.

3.Since the majority element occurs more than all other elements combined, it cannot be completely cancelled out. Therefore, the element left as the candidate at the end must be the majority element.

C++ Code:-

class Solution {
public:
    int majorityElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        int candidate = nums[0];
        int count = 0;

        for (int num : nums) {
            if (count == 0) {
                candidate = num;
            }

            if (num == candidate) {
                count++;
            } else {
                count--;
            }
        }

        return candidate;
    }
};

My approach:-(Better)

1. Store number and it's number of instances in unordered map to reduce time complexity.
2.first the majority element by iterating the map and return the element 

My code (c++):- 

class Solution {
public:
    int majorityElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_map<int,int> umpp ;
       for(int i = 0 ; i < nums.size() ; i++){ 
        umpp[nums[i]]++ ;
        }
        int majorElement = 0;
        int majorElementNum = 0 ;
        for(auto it : umpp){
            if(it.second > majorElement){
                majorElement = it.second;
                majorElementNum = it.first;
            }
        }
        return majorElementNum;
    }
    };